course: world
lesson: 81
title: Magnets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A magnet pulls things made of iron, steel, nickel and cobalt, even without touching them; most other materials, such as wood, plastic, glass, paper, copper and aluminium, are not attracted.
explain: Every magnet has two ends called poles, a north pole and a south pole, where the pull is strongest; two unlike poles attract each other, and two like poles push each other apart.
explain: The force of a magnet can pass through paper, water, wood and even your hand, and it gets weaker the further away you go; the space around a magnet where it pulls is called its magnetic field.
explain: The Earth itself acts like a giant magnet, so a compass needle, which is a small magnet free to turn, always points north and south and helps travellers find their way.
explain: Try it: hold a paper clip under a table and move a magnet on top — the clip follows the magnet, because the magnetic force passes right through the wood.
fact: Magnets attract iron, steel, nickel and cobalt.
fact: Copper, aluminium, gold and silver are metals that magnets do not attract.
fact: Every magnet has a north pole and a south pole.
fact: Like poles repel each other and unlike poles attract.
fact: The pull of a magnet is strongest at its poles.
fact: The pull of a magnet gets weaker with distance.
fact: Magnetic force can pass through paper, wood, water and glass.
fact: The Earth behaves like a giant magnet.
fact: A compass needle is a small magnet that lines up north and south.
fact: If you cut a magnet in half, each piece becomes a magnet with its own two poles.
fact: Electromagnets are made with electricity and can be switched on and off.
exercise: Which of these will a magnet pick up? | A steel paper clip | A plastic spoon from the kitchen | A copper coin | A wooden pencil
exercise: What are the two ends of a magnet called? | The north pole and the south pole | The top end and the bottom end of it | The plus end and the minus end, like a battery | The hot end and the cold end of the bar
exercise: What happens when two north poles are pushed together? | They push each other apart | They pull together tightly | Nothing happens at all | They turn into south poles
exercise: What happens when a north pole meets a south pole? | They pull each other together | They push each other apart | They both lose their magnetism | They start to get hot
exercise: Where is a bar magnet's pull strongest? | At its two ends | In the exact middle | Equally all along it | Only on its top side
exercise: Which metal is not attracted by a magnet? | Aluminium | Iron | Steel | Nickel
exercise: Can a magnet pull a paper clip through a sheet of paper? | Yes, the force passes through paper | No, paper blocks all magnetism like a wall | Only if the paper is wet | Only if the paper is coloured
exercise: What happens to a magnet's pull as you move further away? | It gets weaker | It gets stronger | It stays exactly the same | It changes to a push
exercise: Why does a compass needle point north? | It is a magnet lined up by the Earth's magnetism | The wind always blows its light needle round to the north | The sun pulls it towards itself | It is heavier at one end
exercise: What happens if you cut a bar magnet in half? | Each half becomes a magnet with two poles | One half is north and one half is south only | Both halves stop being magnets | Only the bigger half stays a magnet
exercise: What is the area around a magnet where it pulls called? | Its magnetic field | Its electric circle | Its gravity zone | Its iron cloud
exercise: How is an electromagnet different from a bar magnet? | It can be switched on and off | It attracts only plastic and glass | It has no poles at all at either end | It works only when it is under water
exercise: Which is true about all metals? | Not all metals are attracted by magnets | All metals are attracted by magnets | Only gold is attracted by magnets, and no other metal | No metals are attracted by magnets
exercise: Why does a scrapyard, a yard full of old metal, use an electromagnet on a crane? | It can lift steel and then drop it by switching off | It melts the metal for reuse | It sorts plastic from glass | It is cheaper than a hook
test: You have a mix of steel pins and sand. How could you separate them? | Pull the pins out with a magnet | Pour water to melt the pins | Shake them until the sand floats | Heat them until the sand burns
test: Two ring magnets on a pencil float apart, one above the other. Why? | Like poles are facing each other and repel | Unlike poles are facing each other and attract | The pencil is pushing them up | Air is trapped between them
test: A drinks can is not picked up by a magnet. What is it probably made of? | Aluminium | Steel | Iron | Nickel
test: Which is true? | A magnet can attract a clip through your hand | A magnet works only when it touches things | A magnet pulls harder from far away | A magnet attracts every kind of metal
test: Lost in a forest with no sun, which tool helps you find north? | A compass | A thermometer | A magnifying glass | A ruler
test: You bring a magnet slowly towards a pin. When does the pin jump to it? | When the magnet is close enough for the pull to be strong | Only when the magnet touches it | As soon as the magnet is in the room | Never, pins are not magnetic
test: A pupil says: «A magnet sticks to the fridge, so it will stick to a copper pan too.» What is wrong? | Magnets do not attract copper | Copper is not a metal | Fridges are made of copper | Magnets only stick to fridges
test: What would happen if you broke a magnet into three pieces? | Each piece would have a north and a south pole | Only the middle piece would still be a magnet at all | The pieces would push away all metals near them | The magnet would lose both of its poles for ever
test: Why can a crane drop scrap metal with an electromagnet but not with a bar magnet? | The electromagnet can be switched off | The bar magnet is too weak to lift anything | The electromagnet repels steel | The bar magnet melts the metal
test: magnet won't pick up the coins, why | Some metals, like copper and aluminium, are not attracted by magnets | The coins are too heavy for any magnet | The magnet only works through water | Coins have their own magnet that pushes back
test: snapped my magnet in half lol, is it ruined | No, each piece becomes a magnet with its own two poles | Yes, a broken magnet loses all its pull | Yes, each half now has only one pole, either north or south | No, but only one half still works
test: Mia says magnets stick to all metals. true? | No, they attract iron, steel, nickel and cobalt but not copper or gold | Yes, every metal is pulled by a magnet | Yes, but only if the metal is shiny | No, magnets stick only to steel
test: my mate reckons two north poles snap together. right? | No, like poles repel each other | Yes, like poles always attract | Yes, but only if the magnets are big | No, two north poles do nothing at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the metals attracted by a magnet? | Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt | Copper, aluminium, gold and silver | All metals without exception | No metals, only plastics
exam: It is given that a magnet is divided into two pieces. Determine the consequence for the pieces thereof. | Each piece becomes a magnet with its own two poles | One piece bears only a north pole and the other only a south | Both pieces lose their magnetism entirely | Only the larger piece remains a magnet
exam: Identify the part of a magnet at which its pull is strongest. | At its poles | At its exact middle | Equally along its whole length | At the surface facing upwards
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning electromagnets. | They are made with electricity and may be switched on and off | They are natural stones found in the ground | They remain magnetic for ever once made | They attract copper and aluminium alone
